# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two
# FILES, never a shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is
# defined in run_gates.sh. Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_extractor_js_orm.py" "JS-ORM GATE: PASS" "JS/TS/C# ORM extractor (Drizzle, Mongoose, Sequelize, EF Core, TypeORM explicit)" "Drizzle (pgTable/mysqlTable/sqliteTable), Mongoose (mongoose.model + Schema collection option), Sequelize (sequelize.define + Model.init tableName), EF Core (DbSet<T>), and TypeORM explicit @Entity string name all mint table nodes, lighting up the cross-substrate crown jewel for these high-usage JS/TS/C# stacks." "Adds JS/TS/C# ORM table-declaration patterns to _cg_schema._ORM_PATTERNS so these frameworks mint table nodes (and alters/queries edges) instead of 0. THE GAPS FIXED: (1) Drizzle (fastest-growing TS ORM) -- pgTable/mysqlTable/sqliteTable calls with a string-literal first arg; (2) Mongoose (~4M/wk) -- mongoose.model('Name', schema) model name anchor + new Schema({},{collection:'name'}) explicit collection; (3) Sequelize (~7M/wk) -- sequelize.define('Name',{}) + .init({},{tableName:'name'}) explicit override; (4) EF Core (.NET dominant) -- DbSet<EntityClass> property on a DbContext class, PascalCase type param only (primitives like string/int are filtered by requiring uppercase first letter); (5) TypeORM explicit name precision fix (13-pct miss) -- @Entity('custom_table') now extracts the explicit string BEFORE _ORM_JPA_ENTITY_RE so both the string AND the class name are available as coupling anchors (additive + recall-safe). WHAT THIS GATE PROVES (hermetic, no DB, no Postgres, no network): (1) pgTable/mysqlTable/sqliteTable with a string literal mint the exact table name; (2) mongoose.model first-arg string mints the model name; (3) new Schema collection option mints the explicit collection name; (4) sequelize.define first-arg string mints the model name; (5) .init tableName option mints the explicit table name; (6) DbSet<PascalCase> mints the entity class name lowercased; (7) @Entity('name') mints the explicit string in addition to the class name; (8) PRECISION -- import statements, plain string literals containing ORM function names, and DbSet<lowercase> primitives do NOT mint table nodes; (9) CROSS-SUBSTRATE CROWN JEWEL -- a Drizzle schema file (queries edge) and a SQL migration (alters edge) that both reference the same table name couple via the shared table node with no calls/imports edge between them; (10) never-crash -- empty file, binary-ish content, non-TS files do not raise; (11) content-free -- no file body is emitted. PRECISION GUARDS: Drizzle anchors on the ORM-specific function name at a word boundary followed immediately by a string literal (variable args and import paths do not match); Mongoose anchors on mongoose.model( (not models.model or model()); Sequelize anchors on sequelize.define( and .init( with tableName key inside the options dict; EF Core requires DbSet<T> with uppercase T; TypeORM explicit requires a string literal inside @Entity(). MEASURED on real repos: drizzle-orm 11 -> 169 tables (test schemas + seed types); mongoose 0 -> 133 tables (model names from test suite); typeorm 319 -> 362 tables (91 new @Entity explicit names added to existing class-name matches)." 20
